# Bounded Batches,  Better Books: The Accuracy Case for 15-Invoice Reviews

**By the AmritFlow Team**

Every finance manager knows the 3:30 PM slump in the Accounts Payable department.

An AP specialist is eighty invoices into a dense, flat queue. Their eyes are strained, their attention is slipping, and they are stuck in a relentless "swivel chair" routine—toggling between an attached PDF scan, an Excel tracker, and the ERP screen. Every manual window switch and copied number is another opportunity for a keystroke error.

By the eighty-fifth invoice, mental fatigue is not an ergonomic annoyance—it is a direct balance-sheet liability.

Prolonged, unbroken screen work leads to measurable dips in focus and rising error rates. In high-volume corporate accounting, a tired reviewer misses a mismatched tax ID, overlooks a duplicate bill, or approves an altered line item. On a single ₹10 Lakh supplier bill, an overlooked compliance flaw can put up to ₹1.8 Lakh in Input Tax Credit (ITC) at risk under statutory review.

## The Flawed Industry Pattern: Flat-Queue Overload

Many OCR-first tools default to a single, flat queue built around bulk throughput rather than reviewer ergonomics.

These massive lists treat human reviewers like automated machines that never tire. By presenting hundreds of unorganized files in a continuous stream, they leave clerks to review documents without clear operational boundaries or natural pauses. When software ignores human focus limits, discrepancies slip directly into downstream processes, leaving teams to spend days untangling mismatches during the month-end close.

